Beryllium: shared memory as sealed anonymous memfds with Capsicum rights

Purpose: Phase 2 increment 4 (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md; feature matrix
  S-SHM-01, S-SHM-02; plan/06-SECURITY-MODEL.md section 3.3). On FreeBSD,
  base::subtle::PlatformSharedMemoryRegion::Create() made every region an
  unlinked file in the temporary directory (TMPDIR, usually /tmp): the
  memfd path Chromium uses on Linux is Linux-only, and the imported FreeBSD
  patch only passes the "executable" flag through. The owner's runs of
  0.20.3.4 (5 November 2026) show the result: with inherited descriptors
  gone, each renderer still holds five to nine writable regular files with
  no name, which test_50_sandbox reports. They are these regions. As files
  they are on a disk file system (page content included), and a renderer
  holding one can write it, truncate it (making the browser fault on
  access), grow it until the file system is full, and map it executable.
  Now, on FreeBSD:
  - every non-executable region is a memfd_create() object (a POSIX shared
    memory object with no name, FreeBSD 13 and later), sized with
    ftruncate() and sealed against shrinking and growing (F_SEAL_SHRINK,
    F_SEAL_GROW, F_SEAL_SEAL), as on Linux; no file, no path, no fallback
    to one;
  - the read-only twin of a writable region is a duplicate descriptor
    limited with cap_rights_limit() to CAP_MMAP_R, CAP_FSTAT and CAP_FCNTL
    (fcntls: F_GETFL only), because FreeBSD has no /proc/self/fd to reopen
    a memfd O_RDONLY (and /dev/fd/N shares the open file description, so it
    would still be O_RDWR). The writable descriptor keeps CAP_MMAP_RW,
    CAP_FSTAT and CAP_FCNTL (F_GETFL). Neither can map the region
    executable, truncate it, or change its mode, owner or flags; rights
    travel with a descriptor sent to another process and can only shrink;
  - CheckFDAccessMode() treats an O_RDWR descriptor without CAP_WRITE as
    O_RDONLY, so a region received as read-only is accepted only if its
    rights really forbid writing, and a writable one only if they allow it.
  The Capsicum oracle (freebsd/results/oracle-*-20261101.json, probes
  mem.memfd_seal, mem.ro_twin_map_rw, mem.ro_twin_mprotect,
  mem.ro_twin_map_exec, mem.ro_twin_introspect) shows on 14.5, 15.1 and
  16.0-CURRENT that memfd_create() with seals works in capability mode,
  that such a twin cannot be mapped writable (ENOTCAPABLE) or executable
  (ENOTCAPABLE), that mprotect() cannot make its mapping writable (EACCES),
  and that F_GETFL needs CAP_FCNTL with F_GETFL allowed and then reports
  O_RDWR. Executable regions (no caller in this Chromium asks for one) keep
  the file path.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the imported FreeBSD patch
  patch-base_memory_platform__shared__memory__region__posix.cc (stage 2).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py
  test_renderers_hold_no_directories_or_writable_files (no unnamed
  writable file left in a renderer) and
  test_renderer_shared_memory_is_anonymous_and_limited (renderers hold
  shared memory only as memfd objects, none with the rights to truncate
  or map executable); the whole browser suite, which exercises shared
  memory in every process type.
security-review: required (shared memory between processes of different
  trust)

